# Turning a circle inside out {
  :label: inversion
  :eyebrow: Morsel
}

:config: {
  :citation-style: author-parens-year
  :override-date: 8 November 2026 · 8 minute read
}
::

Fix a circle of radius 1 with center $O$. Inversion in this circle maps each point $P$ other than $O$ to the point $Q$ on the ray from $O$ through $P$ at distance $1/|P|$ from $O$. Points on the circle are fixed, points inside the circle are mapped outside it and points outside are mapped inside, and points close to $O$ are mapped far away from it. Since $1/(1/|P|) = |P|$, inverting $Q$ gives back $P$, and so inversion is its own inverse.

:html: {
  :label: fig-inversion
  :path: figs/inversion.html
  :static: figs/inversion-static.png
  :dark: figs/inversion-static-dark.png
}
:caption: Inversion in the circle of radius 1 with center $O$. The point $Q$ lies on the ray from
$O$ through $P$, at distance $1/|P|$, so $|P|\,|Q| = 1$. Each circle is drawn in the same color as its
image, and the image is dashed.
::

In :ref:fig-inversion::, the point $P$ can be dragged, and $Q$ is redrawn at its inverse. The two points coincide exactly when they lie on the circle.

The product $|P|\,|Q|$ shown under :ref:fig-inversion:: is always equal to 1, as the definition requires. As $P$ approaches $O$, however, $Q$ moves off to infinity, and $O$ itself has no inverse. The standard remedy is to add a single point at infinity to the plane and to define it as the inverse of $O$. With this point added, inversion is a one-to-one map of the extended plane, known as :aside:sphere,the Riemann sphere::.

:aside: {:label: sphere} In complex coordinates with $O$ at $0$, inversion is the map $z \mapsto 1/\bar z$, and its composition with the reflection $z \mapsto \bar z$ is the map $z \mapsto 1/z$. ::

The other two toggles under :ref:fig-inversion:: add two circles, each drawn together with its image: a green circle through $O$, and a blue circle not passing through $O$. The image of the green circle is always a line. This is plausible, since the points of the green circle close to $O$ are mapped far away, and its image must therefore be unbounded. The image of the blue circle, on the other hand, is again a circle. Inversion therefore maps circles and lines to circles and lines, though it does not map centers to centers: the inverse of the center of the blue circle is not the center of its image.

An unbounded image is not necessarily a line, however, so this claim requires proof. The proof of :ref:thm-line, the theorem below::, uses only similar triangles.

:theorem: {
  :label: thm-line
  :nonum:
  :title: Circles through the center
}

  Inversion sends a circle through $O$, with $O$ left out, onto a line that misses $O$.

::

:html: {
  :label: fig-line
  :path: figs/through-line.html
  :static: figs/through-line-static.png
  :dark: figs/through-line-static-dark.png
}
:caption: A circle through $O$, with $A$ the point opposite $O$ and $A'$ the inverse of $A$. As $P$ is
dragged around the circle, its inverse $Q$ remains on the dashed line through $A'$. The triangles $OPA$
and $OA'Q$ are similar, with right angles at $P$ and at $A'$.
::

:proof:

  :step: {:label: stp-line-setup}

    :let: $C$ be a circle through $O$, $A$ the point of $C$ opposite $O$, and $A'$ the inverse
    of $A$. Let $L$ be the line through $A'$ at right angles to $OA$ ::.

  ::

  :step: {:label: stp-line-on}

    :claim: For every point $P$ of $C$ other than $O$, its inverse $Q$ lies on $L$. ::

    :p:

      :step: {:label: stp-line-a} If $P = A$, then $Q = A'$, which lies on $L$. From now on
      $P$ is neither $O$ nor $A$. ::

      :step: {:label: stp-line-thales} The angle $OPA$ is a right angle, since $OA$ is a
      diameter of $C$. ::

      :step: {:label: stp-line-similar} The triangles $OPA$ and $OA'Q$ are similar. They share
      the angle at $O$, and $|OP|\,|OQ| = 1 = |OA|\,|OA'|$ gives $|OP| / |OA'| = |OA| / |OQ|$. ::

      :step: By :ref:stp-line-similar::, the angle $OA'Q$ equals the angle $OPA$, a right angle
      by :ref:stp-line-thales::. So $Q$ lies on the line through $A'$ at right angles to $OA$,
      which is $L$. ::

    ::

  ::

  :step: {:label: stp-line-onto}

    :claim: Every point $X$ of $L$ is the inverse of a point of $C$. ::

    :p: $X$ lies on the same side as $A$ of the line through $O$ at right angles to $OA$, so the
    ray from $O$ through $X$ meets $C$ at a point $P$ other than $O$. By :ref:stp-line-on::, the inverse $Q$ of $P$
    lies on $L$, and it lies on the same ray. The ray meets $L$ only at $X$, so $Q = X$. ::

  ::

  :step: $L$ misses $O$, since $|OA'| = 1 / |OA| > 0$. ::

::

## Beyond a single circle {
  :label: beyond
  :nonum:
}

Inversion is a useful tool in classical geometry for this reason. An inversion centered at the point where two circles are tangent maps them to two parallel lines, and a question about circles packed between the two tangent circles becomes a question about circles packed between two parallel lines, which is often much easier. Many classical results about chains of tangent circles are proved in this way, and several examples can be found in chapter 6 of :cite:coxeter:: and in chapter 5 of :cite:coxetergreitzer::. Inversion is also covered, using complex numbers, in chapter 3 of :cite:needham::.

:html: {
  :label: fig-tangent
  :path: figs/tangent.html
  :static: figs/tangent-static.png
  :dark: figs/tangent-static-dark.png
}
:caption: Inversion centered at $T$, the point where the green and the blue circle are tangent.
Their images are the two dashed parallel lines, and the circle packed between the two circles is
mapped to the dashed circle packed between the lines.
::

Inversion also :aside:liouville,preserves angles::. Two curves crossing at a given angle are mapped to two curves crossing at the same angle, with the orientation reversed. In :ref:fig-angles::, a small square grid near the circle is mapped to a curved grid, and wherever two lines of the grid cross at a right angle, their images cross at a right angle too. Closer to $O$, the image grows and bends more, while the crossings stay right angles.

:html: {
  :label: fig-angles
  :path: figs/angles.html
  :static: figs/angles-static.png
  :dark: figs/angles-static-dark.png
}
:caption: A square grid near the circle and its image under inversion, dashed. The lines of the
image are arcs, and they cross at right angles, as the lines of the grid do.
::

:aside: {:label: liouville} In three or more dimensions there is a strong converse, due to Liouville: every angle-preserving map is a composition of inversions in spheres, reflections and similarities. In the plane there are many more angle-preserving maps, for example every holomorphic function with nonzero derivative. ::

## Try it firsthand {
  :label: try
  :nonum:
}

The tangent circles of :ref:fig-tangent:: lead to a classical theorem of Pappus. A proof by inversion
is sketched after :ref:fig-pappus::, with the justification of each step left open.

:theorem: {
  :label: thm-pappus
  :nonum:
  :title: Pappus chain
}

  Two circles are tangent at $T$, one inside the other, and a chain of circles $C_0, C_1, C_2,
  \ldots$ lies between them, each tangent to both circles and to the next one, with the center of
  $C_0$ on the line through the centers of the two circles. Then the center of $C_n$ lies at
  distance $n\,d_n$ from that line, where $d_n$ is the diameter of $C_n$.

::

:html: {
  :label: fig-pappus
  :path: figs/pappus.html
  :static: figs/pappus-static.png
  :dark: figs/pappus-static-dark.png
}
:caption: A Pappus chain between two circles tangent at $T$, and its image under inversion at $T$,
a stack of equal circles between two parallel lines. The center of the highlighted circle $C_n$
lies at height $n$ times its diameter.
::

In the proof, inversion is taken in the circle centered at $T$ and meeting $C_n$ at right angles, so that $C_n$
is mapped to itself. The two tangent circles are mapped to two parallel lines, at right angles to
the line through their centers, and every circle of the chain is mapped to a circle tangent to both
lines. All the images therefore have the same diameter, the distance between the lines, and each is
tangent to the next. The line through the centers is mapped to itself, and the image of $C_0$ has
its center on it, so the images of $C_0, C_1, \ldots, C_n$ are stacked along the gap and the center
of the image of $C_n$ lies $n$ diameters from that line. Since that image is $C_n$ itself, its
center lies at distance $n\,d_n$.

